problems.problem:
Let $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ be a function such that
(i) for all $x,y\in\mathbb{R}$, $f(x)+f(y)+1\geq f(x+y)\geq f(x)+f(y)$;
(ii) for all $x\in[0,1)$, $f(0)\geq f(x)$;
(iii) $-f(-1)=f(1)=1$.
Find all such functions $f$.

Let $k\geq 14$ be an integer, and let $p_k$ be the largest prime number which is strictly less than $k$. You may assume that $p_k\geq 3k/4$. Let $n$ be a composite integer. Prove:
(a) if $n=2p_k$, then $n$ does not divide $(n-k)!$;
(b) if $n>2p_k$, then $n$ divides $(n-k)!$.

Determine all sequences $a_0, a_1, a_2, \ldots$ of positive integers with $a_0\geq 2015$ such that for all integers $n\geq 1$:
(i) $a_{n+2}$ is divisible by $a_n$;
(ii) $|s_{n+1}-(n+1)a_n|=1$, where $s_{n+1}=a_{n+1}-a_n+a_{n-1}-\cdots+(-1)^{n+1}a_0$.
